{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 32, :comments ["<b>And he ungirded the camels.</b> Rashi, of blessed memory, explained: he untied their muzzle-ropes, which had been sealing their mouths so they would not graze in others' fields. And so the Rabbis, of blessed memory, interpreted in Bereshit Rabbah: 'he untied their muzzle-ropes.' R. Yirmiyah asked R. Ḥiyya bar Abba: 'Were the camels of Abraham not similar to the donkey of R. Pinḥas ben Yair?' And the Ramban, of blessed memory, explained that the intent of this Midrash is to say that it is impossible for the piety in the house of R. Pinḥas ben Yair to be greater than in the house of Abraham; and just as the donkey of R. Pinḥas did not need to be guarded from things forbidden for its owner to feed it, all the more so the camels of the house of Abraham, and there is no need to muzzle them. Therefore he, of blessed memory, explained that the expression 'and he ungirded the camels' means he opened the bonds of their necks, for it was their custom to lead them bound, in the manner of what is said (Isaiah 52:2): ״התפתחי מוסרי צוארך״. And the word ויפתח refers back to Lavan, for he is the one who ungirds and gives straw and feed, and water to wash the feet of the men — end of comment."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:14:48.361299923Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}